.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt PDSELF 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pdself
.Nd create a process descriptor for the calling process
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/procdesc.h
.Ft int
.Fn pdself "int *fdp" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn pdself
system call creates a process descriptor for the calling process and
stores the resulting file descriptor in the integer pointed to by
.Fa fdp .
This is a capability-mode-only system call
.Pq Dv SYF_CAPREQUIRED ;
it returns
.Er ENOTCAPABLE
when called outside
.Xr capsicum 4
capability mode.
.Pp
The resulting process descriptor can be passed over a
.Xr unix 4
domain socket to a supervisor process, which can then use
.Xr pdkill 2 ,
.Xr pdwait 2 ,
and
.Xr pdgetpid 2
to manage the caller without requiring PIDs or traditional UNIX
permission checks.
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
argument may contain the following values:
.Bl -tag -width PD_CLOEXEC
.It Dv PD_CLOEXEC
Set close-on-exec on the returned descriptor.
.It Dv PD_DAEMON
Closing the returned descriptor from another process does not send
.Dv SIGKILL
to the calling process.
Like the other process descriptor flags, this applies only to the
descriptor returned by this call, not to other descriptors for the
same process.
.El
.Pp
At most one process descriptor object exists per process.
If the calling process has none yet,
.Fn pdself
creates one and marks it
.Dv PDF_SELF .
If the calling process already has one, for example because it was
created by
.Xr pdfork 2
or a previous call to
.Fn pdself ,
or because another process obtained one with
.Xr pdopenpid 2 ,
.Fn pdself
returns a new file descriptor referring to the same process descriptor
object, as
.Xr pdopenpid 2
does.
Repeated calls therefore succeed and each returns a distinct file
descriptor.
.Ss Close semantics
The effect of
.Xr close 2
on a process descriptor depends on which process closes it and on the
flags of that particular file:
.Bl -bullet
.It
If the described process itself closes a descriptor for itself, it is
never killed.
If that was the last file referencing the process descriptor, the
descriptor is detached from the process, which from then on behaves
like an ordinary process without a process descriptor.
If, in addition, its parent holds no
.Xr waitpid 2
reference to it (for example because it was created by
.Xr pdfork 2
with
.Dv PD_NOWAITPID ) ,
the process is reparented to its reaper so that its exit status can
still be collected.
.It
If any other process closes a descriptor, and that file was not created
with
.Dv PD_DAEMON ,
the described process receives
.Dv SIGKILL ,
whether or not other descriptors for it remain open.
This also applies when an unreceived descriptor in transit in an
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
message is discarded.
.It
When the last file referencing the process descriptor is closed by a
process other than the described one, the process is detached from the
descriptor and, unless its parent still holds a
.Xr waitpid 2
reference to it, reparented to its reaper so that its exit status is
collected normally.
.It
If the described process has already exited and is a zombie, closing the
last descriptor reaps it, unless its parent still holds a
.Xr waitpid 2
reference; the zombie then remains until the parent collects it.
.El
.Pp
A supervisor that should not terminate the caller merely by closing its
copy of the descriptor needs the caller to have passed a descriptor
created with
.Dv PD_DAEMON .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std
.Sh ERRORS
The
.Fn pdself
system call fails if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The
.Fa fdp
argument points to an invalid address.
The descriptor has nevertheless been allocated and remains open.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The
.Fa flags
argument contains invalid flags.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The per-process file descriptor limit has been reached.
.It Bq Er ENFILE
The system file descriptor limit has been reached.
.It Bq Er ENOTCAPABLE
The caller is not in capability mode.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
The calling process is exiting.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
.Xr close 2 ,
.Xr pdcmp 2 ,
.Xr pdfork 2 ,
.Xr pdgetpid 2 ,
.Xr pdkill 2 ,
.Xr pdwait 2 ,
.Xr capsicum 4 ,
.Xr procdesc 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn pdself
system call first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
